{start_of_verse: Verse 1}
[E]I am an old [A]woman [E]named after my [A]mother.
[E]My old man is [A]another [B]child that's grown [E]old.
[E]If dreams were [A]lightning and [E]thunder were [A]desire
[E]this old house would've [A]burnt down a [B]long time [E]ago.
{end_of_verse}

{start_of_verse: Verse 2}
[E]Make me an [D]angel that [A]flies from [E]Montgomery.
[E]Make me a [D]poster of an [A]old [E]rodeo.
[E]Just give me [D]one thing that [A]I can hold [E]on to.
[E]To believe in this [A]living is just a [B]hard way to [E]go.
{end_of_verse}

{start_of_verse: Verse 3}
When I was a young girl I had me a cowboy,
He wasn’t much to look at, just a free ramblin' man.
But that was a long time, and no matter how I try,
the years just flow by like a broken-down dam.
{end_of_verse}

{start_of_chorus}
There's flies in the kitchen, I can hear all their buzzin'
but I ain't done nothin' since I woke up today.
But how the hell can a person go to work in the morning
come home in the evenin' and have nothin' to say?
{end_of_chorus}
